\description{
One replicate of the simulation study on how the smoothness of the vector
fields affects estimation. Each replicate draws two random ocean-like
fields on a coarse grid over \eqn{[-10, 10]^2}, each made of two eddies
and a meandering Gulf Stream-like current, and turns the grid values into
vector fields three ways:
\itemize{
\item \code{discontinuous}: the value at the nearest grid point (piecewise constant).
\item \code{continuous}: bilinear interpolation (\code{\link[akima:bilinear]{akima::bilinear()}}).
\item \code{differentiable}: bicubic interpolation (\code{\link[akima:bicubic]{akima::bicubic()}}).
}

Then, for every combination of positional error level, true trajectory
complexity and continuity, it simulates a data set and fits FullFours paths
followed by OneShot trajectories with the same fields, recording accuracy,
coverage, interval width and run time. Run it with seeds 1, 2, ..., N to
reproduce the full study. It needs the akima package.

This study is self-contained: everything it needs is defined inside this
function, apart from the package's estimation and evaluation functions and
akima's interpolation.
}
\details{
\strong{Grids.} Each grid has spacing 1 on \eqn{[-10, 10]^2}. Its value at a
grid point is \eqn{10 e_1 + 10 e_2 + 3 g}, where \eqn{e_1, e_2} are eddies
(pure rotations about uniformly placed centres, with random direction,
decaying with distance) and \eqn{g} is a current that follows a randomly
rotated sine curve with random amplitude, frequency, phase and width. The
vectors are then scaled so the longest has length 1. The two grids are
drawn once for the whole replicate.

\strong{Data.} For each setting, true trajectories with the complexity's number
of equally spaced regions on \eqn{[0, t_{end}]} are drawn with
\code{\link[=generateFullTrajectory]{generateFullTrajectory()}} (\code{sigma}, \code{k}). The true path is the
\code{\link[=RungeKutta]{RungeKutta()}} solution through the trajectory-weighted fields of the
setting's continuity, from a start position drawn uniformly on
\code{start_range} in each dimension, with time step \code{t_step}. The fields are
only defined on the grid, so if the path leaves \eqn{[-10, 10]^2}, the
trajectories and start are drawn again (up to \code{max_attempts} times); the
number of draws is recorded. The observed data add independent normal noise
with the error level's standard deviation to each position. All data sets
are simulated (in a fixed order) before any fitting.

\strong{Variants.} Every variant fits a FullFours path and OneShot trajectories
with BIC selection, C1 smoothness and \code{patience}. Each entry of \code{variants}
is one of:
\itemize{
\item \code{"BIC"}: the regions BIC selects, for both the path and the trajectories.
\item \code{"2x BIC path only"}: twice BIC's path regions, BIC's trajectory regions.
\item \code{"2x BIC"}: twice BIC's regions for both (the default undersmoothing).
\item \code{"2x BIC + bootstrap"}: \code{"2x BIC"}, with the node-selection variance from
\code{B} bootstrap replicates added to OneShot's covariance, through the law of
total variance (\code{\link[=getEstimatedTrajectories_Full]{getEstimatedTrajectories_Full()}}).
}

Each setting is fitted with its own continuity's fields.

\strong{Metrics.} For each setting and variant:
\itemize{
\item \code{path_attempts}: the number of draws needed for a path inside the grid.
\item \code{path_rmse}: root mean squared distance between the estimated and true
positions at the data times.
\item \code{traj_avg_sq_dist}: \code{\link[=cubicAvgSquareDist]{cubicAvgSquareDist()}} between the estimated and true
trajectories.
\item \verb{path_coverage_*}: \code{\link[=coverageRatePath]{coverageRatePath()}} of the data by the prediction
intervals, per dimension.
\item \verb{traj_coverage_*}: \code{\link[=coverageRateTraj]{coverageRateTraj()}} of the true trajectories by the
confidence intervals, per trajectory.
\item \verb{path_width_*}: \code{\link[=avgCIWidthPath]{avgCIWidthPath()}} with prediction intervals, per dimension.
\item \verb{traj_width_*}: \code{\link[=avgCIWidthTraj]{avgCIWidthTraj()}}, per trajectory.
\item \verb{traj_coverage_within_*}, \verb{traj_width_within_*}: for \code{"2x BIC + bootstrap"},
the same with OneShot's covariance alone (without the selection part).
\item \code{selection_share}: for \code{"2x BIC + bootstrap"}, the average share of the
trajectory variance that comes from the node selection.
\item \code{time_total}: elapsed seconds for the fit (including the bootstrap).
}

If a fit fails, its metrics are \code{NA} and the error message is recorded.
